</figure>
<figure id="S1.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Conditions for Beneficial Spike Alignment at Finite Aspect Ratios (<math id="S1.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=d/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=d/n</annotation></semantics></math>).<span id="S1.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> This table outlines the specific regions where alignment of the target signal with the data’s principal spike direction improves generalization. Conditions depend on the problem setting (well-specified vs. mis-specified), the spike scaling regime (operator or frobenius norm based), the overparameterization level <math id="S1.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=d/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=d/n</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative dependence of the targets <math id="S1.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> on the spike versus the bulk <math id="S1.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S1.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"><span id="S1.T2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting</span></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"><span id="S1.T2.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment Beneficial Region</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T2.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T2.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Well-Specified, Operator Norm</td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t"></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S1.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;c(c-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;c(c-2)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T2.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T2.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;">Well-Specified, Frobenius Norm</td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.3.2" class="ltx_td" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"><math id="S1.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T2.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T2.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;">Misspecified, No Covariate Shift, Operator Norm</td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.4.2" class="ltx_td" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 10.0pt;"><math id="S1.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq\frac{1}{c}\left(\frac{3c^{2}-\gamma+2c\gamma-2c}{(c^{2}+\gamma)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq\frac{1}{c}\left(\frac{3c^{2}-\gamma+2c\gamma-2c}{(c^{2}+\gamma)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T2.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T2.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Misspecified, No Covariate Shift, Frobenius Norm</td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb"></td>
<td id="S1.T2.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S1.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{c}&lt;\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}&lt;2-\frac{1}{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{c}&lt;\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}&lt;2-\frac{1}{c}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem Setting</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the generalization of minimum-norm interpolators in high-dimensional linear regression. Using a spiked covariance data model, we quantify how spike strength and alignment influence generalization and the emergence of benign, tempered, or catastrophic overfitting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Model.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a data matrix <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}={\bm{Z}}+{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}={\bm{Z}}+{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">signal component</em> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">isotropic noise component</em> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy the following assumptions. Specifically, we shall that the population feature covariance is <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Sigma}}=\theta^{2}{\bm{u}}{\bm{u}}^{\top}+\tau^{2}{\bm{I}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Sigma}}=\theta^{2}{\bm{u}}{\bm{u}}^{\top}+\tau^{2}{\bm{I}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, modeling a rank-one perturbation of isotropic noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Signal)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed unit vector representing the spike direction. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}=\theta{\bm{u}}{\bm{v}}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}=\theta{\bm{u}}{\bm{v}}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the spike strength, and the vector <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has i.i.d. standard normal entries.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Noise)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The entries of <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> have zero mean and variance <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies:</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its entries are uncorrelated and possess finite fourth moments.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its distribution is invariant under left and right orthogonal transformations.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The empirical spectral distribution of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\tau^{2}d}{\bm{A}}{\bm{A}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\tau^{2}d}{\bm{A}}{\bm{A}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges to the Marchenko–Pastur law as </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,d\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,d\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/n\to c\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/n\to c\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spike Strength Normalizations.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two key scaling regimes for the spike strength relative to the bulk noise. These lead to distinct generalization behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operator Norm Scaling (<math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
Here <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> tunes the spike strength <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the noise variance <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the spectral norm of the signal component <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is comparable to that of the noise component <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the spike emerges as an isolated eigenvalue beyond the bulk spectrum established by <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a phenomenon known as the Baik–Ben Arous–Péché (BBP) transition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>)</cite>. This scaling reflects spikes in learned neural network features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frobenius Norm Scaling (<math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
Here <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> matches expected signal and noise Frobenius norms (<math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|{\bm{Z}}\|_{F}^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|{\bm{A}}\|_{F}^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|{\bm{Z}}\|_{F}^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|{\bm{A}}\|_{F}^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>) and the spike has macroscopic proportion of the energy. Such strong signals can lead to improved sample complexity, potentially overcoming limitations observed in purely isotropic models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target Model.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{i}={\bm{z}}_{i}+{\bm{a}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{i}={\bm{z}}_{i}+{\bm{a}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the targets <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{i}=\alpha_{Z}{\bm{z}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\alpha_{A}{\bm{a}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{i}=\alpha_{Z}{\bm{z}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\alpha_{A}{\bm{a}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in uniformly distributed in the subspace <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bm{\beta}}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}:{\bm{\beta}}^{\top}{\bm{u}}=\text{ fixed constant}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext> fixed constant</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bm{\beta}}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}:{\bm{\beta}}^{\top}{\bm{u}}=\text{ fixed constant}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true underlying parameter vector. The terms <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th columns of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. The observation noise <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}^{2}]=\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[{\bm{\varepsilon}}_{i}^{2}]=\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The coefficients <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z},\alpha_{A}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z},\alpha_{A}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> control the target’s dependence on the signal and noise components. If <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the true data generating process for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> differentially weights components of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, causing model misspecification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization Risk.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the minimum-norm interpolating ordinary least squares estimator:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{int}={\bm{X}}^{\dagger}{\bm{y}},\qquad\text{ with }\qquad\hat{{\bm{y}}}=(\tilde{{\bm{z}}}+\tilde{{\bm{a}}}){\bm{\beta}}_{int}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext> with </mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{int}={\bm{X}}^{\dagger}{\bm{y}},\qquad\text{ with }\qquad\hat{{\bm{y}}}=(\tilde{{\bm{z}}}+\tilde{{\bm{a}}}){\bm{\beta}}_{int}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pseudoinverse. Given a new test data point <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{x}}}=\tilde{{\bm{z}}}+\tilde{{\bm{a}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{x}}}=\tilde{{\bm{z}}}+\tilde{{\bm{a}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and targets <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{y}}}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}\tilde{{\bm{z}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\tilde{\alpha}_{A}\tilde{{\bm{a}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{y}}}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}\tilde{{\bm{z}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\tilde{\alpha}_{A}\tilde{{\bm{a}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with potentially with different coefficients <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}_{Z},\tilde{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}_{Z},\tilde{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and model parameters <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\tau},\tilde{\tau_{\varepsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\tau},\tilde{\tau_{\varepsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the generalization risk is defined as the expected squared prediction error:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}},\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\}}\left[(\tilde{{\bm{y}}}-\hat{{\bm{y}}})^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}},\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\}}\left[(\tilde{{\bm{y}}}-\tilde{{\bm{x}}}^{T}{\bm{\beta}}_{int})^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}},\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\}}\left[(\tilde{{\bm{y}}}-\hat{{\bm{y}}})^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}},\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\}}\left[(\tilde{{\bm{y}}}-\tilde{{\bm{x}}}^{T}{\bm{\beta}}_{int})^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The expectation is over the training data <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{X}},{\bm{\varepsilon}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the test data realization <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\{\tilde{{\bm{x}}},\tilde{{\bm{\varepsilon}}}\})</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall denote the asymptotic excess risk in the proportional regime as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}=\lim_{n,d\to\infty,\,d/n\to c}\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})-\tilde{\tau}^{2}_{\varepsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}=\lim_{n,d\to\infty,\,d/n\to c}\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})-\tilde{\tau}^{2}_{\varepsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalizing Prior Work)<span id="Thmremark1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This problem formulation encompasses several existing models as special cases. For instance, isotropic regression settings studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> are recovered by setting <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=0</annotation></semantics></math> (no spike) and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Spike recovery models, such as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, correspond to specific choices like <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}=1/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}=1/d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Our generalized setup allows for a nuanced investigation of the interplay between signal structure, target alignment, and overparameterization.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantifying the Benefit of Alignment.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key aspect of our investigation is to determine when the alignment of the true parameter vector <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with the data’s principal spike direction <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is beneficial for generalization. We define alignment as <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">beneficial</em> if the generalization risk <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}({\bm{\beta}}_{int})</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>), is monotonically decreasing as a function of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, alignment is <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detrimental</em> if the risk is a monotonically increasing function of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Characterizing Overfitting Regimes.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we classify the asymptotic behavior of the excess risk, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as benign, tempered or catastrophic.
We say the overfitting is <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">benign</span> if <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tempered</span> if this limit is positive and finite, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">catastrophic</span> if this limit is infinite.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Theoretical Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our core theoretical contribution is a precise analytical formula for excess risk in the spiked covariance model. This result relies on Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which encompasses both the operator norm scaling
(<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Frobenius norm scaling (<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)
regimes. We develop our general risk theorem by analyzing progressively complex scenarios. Specifically, our forthcoming theorems provide specific conditions for benign, tempered, or catastrophic overfitting (as <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>), and determine when, for finite <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with spike
<math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is beneficial or detrimental.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scaling)<span id="Thmassumption3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,d\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,d\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/n\to c\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/n\to c\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that
<math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\tau^{2})\leq\theta^{2}\leq O(d\tau^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\tau^{2})\leq\theta^{2}\leq O(d\tau^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}=\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}=\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Well Specified Problem</h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/error_vs_c-operator-norm.png" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Operator norm scaling (<math id="S3.F1.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=c\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=c\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Alignment initially improves generalization, but have catastrophic risk as <math id="S3.F1.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Anti-alignment yields tempered risk.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/error_vs_c-frobenius-norm-2.png" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Equal Frobenius norm scaling (<math id="S3.F1.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Alignment leads to benign overfitting, while anti-alignment results in tempered risk.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Excess error vs. overparameterization ratio <math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=d/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=d/n</annotation></semantics></math> in the well-specified case. Each plot shows the risk for aligned and anti-aligned targets under different spike scaling regimes. <span id="S3.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The scatter plots are empirically obtained and the lines are theory.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by analyzing the well-specified case, where the target <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct linear function of the observed covariates <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}={\bm{Z}}+{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}={\bm{Z}}+{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This scenario is realized by setting:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=\alpha{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=\alpha{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}+\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the model is properly specified.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Well-Specified Risk)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given data <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{X}},{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{X}},{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{{\bm{X}}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{{\bm{X}}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}})</annotation></semantics></math> generated according to Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Signal). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (Signal), <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Noise). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Noise), Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Target Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Target Model), and Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (Scaling). If the well-specification condition <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> holds, the asymptotic excess risk <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}&amp;\text{if }c&lt;1\\
\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}+\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left[\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\theta^{2}\tau^{2}c^{2}-2\theta^{2}\tau^{2}c-\theta^{4}}{(\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c)^{2}}\right]&amp;\text{if }c&gt;1\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}&amp;\text{if }c&lt;1\\
\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}+\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left[\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\theta^{2}\tau^{2}c^{2}-2\theta^{2}\tau^{2}c-\theta^{4}}{(\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c)^{2}}\right]&amp;\text{if }c&gt;1\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit vector defining the spike direction.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (a regime not allowed by Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> but useful for sanity checks), the coefficient of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛃</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, the risk expression aligns with that of isotropic models, such as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 1)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operator Norm Scaling (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this regime, the excess risk for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\frac{\gamma c^{2}-2\gamma c-\gamma^{2}}{(\gamma+c)^{2}}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\,\frac{1}{c-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\frac{\gamma c^{2}-2\gamma c-\gamma^{2}}{(\gamma+c)^{2}}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\,\frac{1}{c-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The formula shows that alignment with the spike direction <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is beneficial if and only if the coefficient of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is negative, which occurs when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;c(c-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;c(c-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider different scalings for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 1: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (constant with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
The condition for beneficial alignment, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;c(c-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;c(c-2)</annotation></semantics></math>, interacts intricately with the BBP phase transition condition, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}\approx 4.212" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>4.212</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}\approx 4.212</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique solution to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(c-2)=(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(c-2)=(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;c&lt;c_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;c&lt;c_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>: Here, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(c-2)&lt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(c-2)&lt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(c-2)&lt;\gamma&lt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(c-2)&lt;\gamma&lt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment is beneficial even though the BBP transition has <em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> occurred (the spike is not resolved from the bulk).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;c_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;c_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>: Here, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(c-2)&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(c-2)&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For alignment to be beneficial (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;c(c-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;c(c-2)</annotation></semantics></math>), the BBP transition must have occurred (as <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;c(c-2)\implies\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;c(c-2)\implies\gamma&gt;(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). However, the BBP transition occurring is not sufficient for beneficial alignment. If <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}&lt;\gamma&lt;c(c-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>c</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\sqrt{c})^{2}&lt;\gamma&lt;c(c-2)</annotation></semantics></math>, the BBP transition occurs, yet alignment is detrimental.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Regarding the type of overfitting as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (while <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> remains constant):</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\,\tau^{2}\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\gamma({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\,\tau^{2}\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\gamma({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Since this limit is a positive constant, we consistently observe <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tempered overfitting</em> when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 2: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\omega_{c}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\omega_{c}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> grows with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
The behavior depends on the growth rate of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. The limit of the excess risk for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\cdot\begin{cases}\infty&amp;\text{if }\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})\\
\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+(\frac{1}{\phi}-1)({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}&amp;\text{if }\gamma=\phi c^{2}\text{ for const. }\phi&gt;0\\
\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}&amp;\text{if }\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϕ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for const. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\cdot\begin{cases}\infty&amp;\text{if }\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})\\
\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+(\frac{1}{\phi}-1)({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}&amp;\text{if }\gamma=\phi c^{2}\text{ for const. }\phi&gt;0\\
\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}&amp;\text{if }\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, while <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> gives tempered overfitting, increasing spike strength to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> results in <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">catastrophic overfitting</em>, even though morally, this version of the problem has less noise. Additionally, we see that this catastrophic overfitting is not present in the anti-aligned (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> case. More, aligned with intuition, we see that further increasing the size of the spike improves the generalization performance. Specifically, we get <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tempered overfitting</em> if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\phi c^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\phi c^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benign overfitting</em> if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=c</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> coefficient is <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c-3)/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c-3)/4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;c&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;c&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment is beneficial and for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment becomes detrimental. As <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk grows approximately as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\frac{c}{4}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\frac{c}{4}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">catastrophic overfitting</em>. In contrast, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk grows like <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}(1-1/c)\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}(1-1/c)\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tempered overfitting</em>. This transition is illustrated in <a href="#S3.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Well Specified Problem ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Frobenius Norm Scaling (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The excess risk for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> simplifies to:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c&gt;1}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\,\frac{1}{c-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c&gt;1}=\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)\left(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\,\frac{1}{c-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We have a few observations. First, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\parallel{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> tends to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benign overfitting</em>). Second, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is not perfectly aligned with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}\to\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}\to\alpha^{2}\tau^{2}(\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}-({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tempered overfitting</em>). Finally, the coefficient of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the risk formula is negative. Hence, in contrast with the operator norm regime, <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment is always beneficial</em> in this regime for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and we visualize these behaviors in <a href="#S3.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Well Specified Problem ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Takeaways for the Well-Specified Case.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spike scaling profoundly impacts overfitting, especially with target alignment. For aligned targets, increasing spike strength can drive transitions from tempered <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> catastrophic <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> tempered <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> benign overfitting, while anti-alignment (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>) can mitigate catastrophic overfitting. Additionally, alignment with the spike is not always beneficial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next consider misspecified targets <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> with differing dependence on spike <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and noise <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> feature components. Specifically, we assume <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> but introduce no covariate shift between training and test distributions, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}_{A}=\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This scenario models situations where intrinsic feature properties lead to differential correlations with the target, a common occurrence in practice.
For notational convenience, we define <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{c}:=\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{c}:=\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}:=\alpha_{Z}-\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}:=\alpha_{Z}-\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Misspecified)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Signal). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Noise). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Target Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. If <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> holds with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}+\tau^{2}\,({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\Delta_{1}^{2}}{1-c}\,\frac{\theta^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}}&amp;c&lt;1\\[10.0pt]
\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}+\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+\tau^{2}\,({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\Delta_{c}^{2}\,\frac{\theta^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c}\left[\frac{c}{c-1}\,\frac{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c}-2\frac{\alpha_{A}}{\Delta_{c}}\right]&amp;c&gt;1\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}+\tau^{2}\,({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\Delta_{1}^{2}}{1-c}\,\frac{\theta^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}}&amp;c&lt;1\\[10.0pt]
\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}+\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+\tau^{2}\,({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\Delta_{c}^{2}\,\frac{\theta^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c}\left[\frac{c}{c-1}\,\frac{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c^{2}}{\theta^{2}+\tau^{2}c}-2\frac{\alpha_{A}}{\Delta_{c}}\right]&amp;c&gt;1\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key observation is that misspecification (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>) can itself induce double descent, even if <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This contrasts with the well-specified case where, if <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, double descent is absent. However, in the misspecified case, we do not observe double descent if there is no alignment <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}^{\top}_{*}{\bm{u}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}^{\top}_{*}{\bm{u}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/neg-miss-error_vs_c-operator-norm-alpha_A-2-alpha_Z-1-3.png" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Under operator norm scaling (<math id="S3.F2.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=c\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=c\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) with <math id="S3.F2.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F2.sf1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment initially improves generalization for small <math id="S3.F2.sf1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, but becomes harmful beyond a critical point, leading to catastrophic overfitting.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/neg-miss-error_vs_c-frobenius-norm-alpha_A-1-alpha_Z-1.1.png" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Under Frobenius norm scaling (<math id="S3.F2.sf2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\sqrt{d}\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\sqrt{d}\tau</annotation></semantics></math>) with <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=1.1</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment remains better than anti-alignment across all <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, but benign overfitting is not achieved unless <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Transition from beneficial to harmful alignment under mild misspecification. <span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The scatter plots are empirically obtained and the lines are theory.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equal Operator Norm Case.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=\gamma\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\Delta_{1}^{2}}{1-c}\,\frac{\gamma}{\gamma+1}+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}&amp;c&lt;1\\
\tau^{2}\frac{\gamma}{\gamma+c}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\Delta_{c}^{2}\left[\left(\frac{c^{2}+\gamma}{\gamma+c}\,\frac{c}{c-1}\right)-2\frac{\alpha_{A}}{\Delta_{c}}\right]+\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}&amp;c&gt;1\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\,\frac{\Delta_{1}^{2}}{1-c}\,\frac{\gamma}{\gamma+1}+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{c}{1-c}&amp;c&lt;1\\
\tau^{2}\frac{\gamma}{\gamma+c}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\Delta_{c}^{2}\left[\left(\frac{c^{2}+\gamma}{\gamma+c}\,\frac{c}{c-1}\right)-2\frac{\alpha_{A}}{\Delta_{c}}\right]+\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}\frac{1}{c-1}&amp;c&gt;1\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the spike is <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detrimental</em>. For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the behavior depends on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq\frac{1}{c}\left(\frac{3c^{2}-\gamma+2c\gamma-2c}{(c^{2}+\gamma)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq\frac{1}{c}\left(\frac{3c^{2}-\gamma+2c\gamma-2c}{(c^{2}+\gamma)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">then we have that the coefficient in front of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}^{\top}_{*}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}^{\top}_{*}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is negative. Thus, when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> lies between these thresholds, the spike <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">helps</em>, but the spike <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is harmful</em> outside this range. As <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=o_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=o_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the beneficial region shrinks and <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment increasingly harms generalization</em>. On the other hand, if the spike is big enough (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>), we have that the beneficial region limits to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Figures <a href="#S3.F3.sf1" title="Figure 3(a) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ Equal Frobenius Norm Case. ‣ 3.2 Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F3.sf2" title="Figure 3(b) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ Equal Frobenius Norm Case. ‣ 3.2 Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> plot the coefficient of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=20</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The upper bound on beneficial <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is surprising, as stronger target dependence on the spike might be expected to always favor alignment. Additionally, the dependence on the level of overparameterization <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> also offers new insights. Consider the example of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=c</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;(9+\sqrt{57})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>57</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;(9+\sqrt{57})/2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that the ratio is outside the beneficial region. Figure <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> shows that in the beneficial region, the aligned risk is lower than the anti-aligned risk. However, outside the beneficial region, the aligned risk becomes strictly larger than the anti-aligned counterpart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, in terms of benign vs. tempered vs. catastrophic overfitting, we have that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}\left[\gamma\alpha_{Z}^{2}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}+\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\right]&{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)\\[5.0pt]
\infty&{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})\\[5.0pt]
\tau^{2}\left[\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\left(\alpha_{Z}^{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{\phi}\right)-2\alpha_{Z}\alpha_{A}\right)({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right]&{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\phi c^{2}\\[5.0pt]
\tau^{2}(\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+(\alpha_{Z}^{2}-2\alpha_{Z}\alpha_{A})({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2})&{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})\\[5.0pt]
\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}&{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϕ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\begin{cases}\tau^{2}\left[\gamma\alpha_{Z}^{2}({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}+\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\right]&amp;{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\Theta_{c}(1)\\[5.0pt]
\infty&amp;{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})\\[5.0pt]
\tau^{2}\left[\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+\left(\alpha_{Z}^{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{\phi}\right)-2\alpha_{Z}\alpha_{A}\right)({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\right]&amp;{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\phi c^{2}\\[5.0pt]
\tau^{2}(\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}+(\alpha_{Z}^{2}-2\alpha_{Z}\alpha_{A})({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2})&amp;{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}},\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})\\[5.0pt]
\alpha_{A}^{2}\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}&amp;{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\perp{\bm{u}}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> we have <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">catastrophic overfitting</em>. If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\Theta_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\Theta_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, overfitting is tempered, with benign overfitting precluded (Appendix <a href="#Thmprop3" title="Proposition 3 (Non–existence of a canceling scale parameter). ‣ F.4 Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix F Proof of Specific Cases and Overfitting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). If <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\omega_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, overfitting is again tempered with benign requiring returning to the well-specified case (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equal Frobenius Norm Case.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{2}=d\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c&gt;1}=\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\left[\frac{c}{c-1}\left(\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}\right)^{2}-2\alpha_{A}\left(\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}\right)\right]+\frac{\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{c-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c&gt;1}=\alpha_{A}^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{c}\right)+({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}\left[\frac{c}{c-1}\left(\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}\right)^{2}-2\alpha_{A}\left(\alpha_{Z}-\frac{\alpha_{A}}{c}\right)\right]+\frac{\tau_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{c-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the beneficial region for the ratio <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq 2-\frac{1}{c}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{c}\leq\frac{\alpha_{Z}}{\alpha_{A}}\leq 2-\frac{1}{c}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The beneficial region expands with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, making alignment increasingly beneficial in extreme overparameterization (Figure <a href="#S3.F3.sf3" title="Figure 3(c) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ Equal Frobenius Norm Case. ‣ 3.2 Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(c)</span></a>). Beneficial alignment can also be seen in <a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Misspecified Case and no Covariate Shift ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>. Here <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}=1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}=1.1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is in the beneficial region for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;10/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;10/9</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, the overfitting is tempered unless <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}=\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}=\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/operator_norm_c_2.0.png" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/401;" width="476" height="401" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Operator norm scaling<span id="S3.F3.sf1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, <math id="S3.F3.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Large beneficial region. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/operator_norm_c_20.0.png" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/409;" width="476" height="409" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Operator norm scaling<span id="S3.F3.sf2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, <math id="S3.F3.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=20</annotation></semantics></math>. Smaller beneficial region</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/frobenius_norm_c_1000.0.png" id="S3.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/390;" width="476" height="390" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Frobenius norm scaling<span id="S3.F3.sf3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, <math id="S3.F3.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1000</annotation></semantics></math>. The beneficial region persists at extreme overparameterization.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase boundaries for spike alignment impact.</span> Coefficient of <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}/\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating whether alignment improves or harms generalization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Misspecified Target and Covariate Shift</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, in addition to misspecifation, we also have covariate shift between train and test. Specifically, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we have the spike/noise importance differ between train and test. For the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equal operator norm</span> case, we show the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given data <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Signal). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Noise). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Target Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. If <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> holds, catastrophic overfitting occurs if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{c}(1)\leq\gamma\leq o_{c}(c^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}\neq\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}\neq\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\omega_{c}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\omega_{c}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⟂̸</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\not\perp{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> we get catastrophic overfitting. Other scenarios yield tempered overfitting.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Different covariate shifts pose varying challenges. In particular, if <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, (target’s spike dependence shifts), then catastrophic overfitting becomes unavoidable for sufficiently large spikes. This contradicts the earlier theoretical intuition, as increasing the spike size in this setting actually induces catastrophic overfitting instead of mitigating it.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equal Frobenius Norm.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, we have the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}},\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Signal). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}},\tilde{{\bm{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Noise). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}},\tilde{{\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Target Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. If <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> holds and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{c}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{c}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\tau^{2}\left[({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}(\alpha_{Z}^{2}-2\tilde{\alpha}_{A}\alpha_{Z})+\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\tilde{\alpha}_{A}^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{c\to\infty}\mathcal{R}_{c}=\tau^{2}\left[({\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}{\bm{u}})^{2}(\alpha_{Z}^{2}-2\tilde{\alpha}_{A}\alpha_{Z})+\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}\tilde{\alpha}_{A}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}\neq\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, catastrophic overfitting occurs. When <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> are parallel, we have that
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}(\alpha_{Z}-\tilde{\alpha}_{A})^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}\|^{2}(\alpha_{Z}-\tilde{\alpha}_{A})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
This is benign if and only if <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, if training data is misspecified (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}\neq\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}\neq\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>) but test data is well-specified and matches the training spike dependence (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{Z}=\tilde{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>), benign overfitting becomes achievable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>General Theorem</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior results are special cases of our main theorem (<a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5 (Generalization Risk). ‣ 3.4 General Theorem ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Its full form is complex (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proof of Theorem ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). We present a high-level decomposition here.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalization Risk)<span id="Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Signal). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Noise). ‣ Data Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Scaling). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> hold.</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\mathbb{E}\left[\underbrace{\left\|\tilde{\alpha}_{z}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}-{\bm{\beta}}_{int}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Bias}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\left\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Variance}}+\underbrace{\tilde{\alpha}_{A}^{2}\left\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Data Noise}}+\underbrace{\left(-2\tilde{\alpha}_{A}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\right)}_{\text{Target Alignment}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Bias</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Variance</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Data Noise</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Target Alignment</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\mathbb{E}\left[\underbrace{\left\|\tilde{\alpha}_{z}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}-{\bm{\beta}}_{int}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{Z}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Bias}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\left\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Variance}}+\underbrace{\tilde{\alpha}_{A}^{2}\left\|{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\right\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{Data Noise}}+\underbrace{\left(-2\tilde{\alpha}_{A}{\bm{\beta}}_{*}^{\top}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}\tilde{{\bm{A}}}^{\top}{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\right)}_{\text{Target Alignment}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bias.</span>
This is the squared error between the learned predictor <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{int}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{int}</annotation></semantics></math> and the true parameter <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">projected onto the spike direction</em> <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, the risk penalizes discrepancies only along the top eigen-direction of the population covariance <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting the anistropic influence of the spike.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variance.</span>
The variance is equivalent to <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This mirrors classical isotropic regression results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>)</cite>, but the norm <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\beta}}_{int}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> itself is dependent upon the interaction between signal and noise, the alignment between <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the scaling parameters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Noise.</span>
The data noise term quantifies the contribution of the noise matrix <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target outputs <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Even in the absence of observation noise (<math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}_{\varepsilon}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), target corruption via data noise can create an irreducible error floor.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Alignment.</span>
The alignment term measures the inner product between <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{int}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{int}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the sample noise covariance.
This cross-term captures how mismatch between <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{int}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{int}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, especially when mediated by <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, can amplify or dampen generalization error.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Extension: Nonlinear Models Also Exhibit Alignment Phase Transitions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our theoretical focus is on linear regression, key phenomena like <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> dependent non-monotonic alignment effects appear in nonlinear models as well. We test this by training 3-layer ReLU networks to predict <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Target Model. ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) given <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we vary the alignment angle between spike <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and record the generalization error. Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.5 Extension: Nonlinear Models Also Exhibit Alignment Phase Transitions ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, shows our results for three <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> values. For <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing alignment with the spike is detrimental. For <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment is beneficial, while for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, alignment is detrimental again. This mirrors our theoretical findings that there is a region for beneficial alignment and a nuanced phase transition for different <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> values.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/error_vs_angle_plot-small-alpha_z-3-network.png" id="S3.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/284;" width="476" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="S3.F4.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, alignment helps.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/error_vs_angle_plot-medium-alpha_z-3-network.png" id="S3.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/284;" width="476" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="S3.F4.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, mixed behavior.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01414v1/figs/error_vs_angle_plot-large-alpha_z-3-network.png" id="S3.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/284;" width="476" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="S3.F4.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, alignment hurts.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment-phase transitions persist in deep networks.</span>
Generalization error vs. angle between spike direction <math id="S3.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and ground-truth parameter <math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\beta}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\beta}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> when fitting data with a 3-layer ReLU networks. The effect of alignment switches as <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, consistent with the phase transitions predicted by our theory. Experimental details are in Appendix<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Non-Linear Experiment ‣ Risk Phase Transitions in Spiked Regression: Alignment Driven Benign and Catastrophic Overfitting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work provided a precise analytical characterization of the generalization error for minimum-norm interpolators in spiked covariance models. We decomposed the risk into interpretable components and comprehensively classified overfitting regimes based on spike strength, target alignment, and overparameterization. We reveal surprising phenomena, such as the potential for increasing spike strength to induce catastrophic overfitting before benign overfitting in well-specified aligned problems, and that strong target-spike alignment is not universally beneficial, especially under model misspecification. These alignment-dependent phase transitions, theoretically derived for linear models, were also empirically observed in nonlinear neural networks, suggesting broader relevance. Our results offer a more nuanced understanding of generalization in the presence of data anisotropy, challenging conventional intuitions and providing a detailed map of risk behaviors in overparameterized settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
